{"step_index":213,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T13:27:59Z","thinking":"**Identifying Institution Discrepancy**\n\nExamination of the fraud detection service reveals hardcoded institution values. One instance incorrectly reports \"University of Tech & Science\" for Sarah Jenkins. This contradicts the data initializer, which assigns her institution as \"National Institute of Technology.\" A similar discrepancy exists for Marcus Vance.\n\n\n**Refactoring for Data Accuracy**\n\nThe current implementation relies on hardcoded responses, causing incorrect and inconsistent information. Replacing these with database queries will provide accurate and up-to-date details. This includes retrieving details like student's real name, institution, award date, and status from the repository, ensuring all responses align with the real data.\n\n\n**Defining Access Levels Precisely**\n\nThe System Administrator (`ROLE_ADMIN`) requires comprehensive access across all system features, including full telemetry, audit trails, credential management (issuance and revocation), qualifications registry, and blockchain explorer functions. The Officer role accesses some subset of these features. Verification is available for another limited set of features.\n\n\n**Mapping Access by Role**\n\nA systematic approach is needed to define access privileges for each user role, explicitly outlining what they can and cannot do within the system. This includes specifying data and feature access, covering everything from validation processes to the fraud scanner. The intended access levels require verification and clarification to ensure they are complete and align with the initial requirements.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\Madziva\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\scratch\\\\qualification-verification-system\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\com\\\\devops\\\\qvs\\\\controller\\\\QualificationController.java\"","End