{"step_index":221,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T13:28:46Z","thinking":"**Identifying Missing CSS Class**\n\nExamining `style.css` reveals the absence of a `.container` rule, despite its presence in the HTML. This suggests a missing style definition for a frequently used container element. Adding the CSS rule defines the necessary layout and spacing for content within a container element, fixing the missing style.\n\n\n**Simplifying Verification Button Placement**\n\nAnalyzing user feedback and examining the welcome page's HTML reveals a potential usability issue. The current presentation includes two buttons, which may confuse users into believing dashboard access is possible without login. The goal is to provide a single, clear button on the welcome page directing the user to login for verification.\n\n\n**Analyzing Access Levels and Roles**\n\nExamining the login page's pre-configured demo accounts (verifier, officer) strongly suggests distinct access levels. The goal is to verify and, if necessary, correct the access control mechanisms between admin, officer, and verifier roles, as implied by the differing demo accounts. Further investigation is required to see whether access controls between different roles are working as expected.\n\n\n**Visualizing Access Control Matrix**\n\nImplementing a permission scope matrix on the login screen appears crucial for clear role distinction. This design will explicitly define permitted and restricted functionalities under each account button (verifier, officer, admin). The aim is to create a visually accessible interface, clearly depicting access privileges for each user role.\n\n\n**Implementing Role-Based UI Elements**\n\nObserving the provided `dashboard.html` requirements necessitates dynamic UI changes based on user roles. The objective is to configure tab visibility, button availability, and informational banner text reflecting role-specific access rights, preventing unauthorized features. This includes hiding restricted actions such as issuing or revoking credentials for non-administrative users.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\Madziva\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\scratch\\\\qualification-verification-system\\\\src\\\\main\\\\java\\\\com\\\\devops\\\\qvs\\\\repository\\\\QualificationRepository.java\"","toolAction":"\"Viewing QualificationRepository.java\"","toolSummary":"\"View QualificationRepository.java\""}}]}
